## V Stability analysis

Consider the following two coupled differential equations:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x} &= f(x,y) \\
\dot{y} &= g(x,y)
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.1}
$$

The nullclines are defined as:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x} &= 0 \rightarrow f(x_o, y_o) = 0 \\
\dot{y} &= 0 \rightarrow g(x_o, y_o) = 0
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.2}
$$

in order to solve [V.2] we linearize around the fixed point $(x_o, y_o)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{x} &\equiv x - x_o \\
\tilde{y} &\equiv y - y_o
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.3}
$$

If $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ are approximated by a first order Taylor expansion, [V.2] can be written as:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot{\tilde{x}} &\approx \tilde{x} \left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_o, y_o)} + \tilde{y} \left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_o, y_o)} \equiv a\tilde{x} + b\tilde{y} \\
\dot{\tilde{y}} &\approx \tilde{x} \left.\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_o, y_o)} + \tilde{y} \left.\frac{\partial g}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_o, y_o)} \equiv c\tilde{x} + d\tilde{y}
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.4}
$$

or in matrix notation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot{\vec{X}} &= A\vec{X} \\
A &= \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{bmatrix} \\
\dot{\vec{X}} &= \begin{bmatrix} \dot{\tilde{x}} \\ \dot{\tilde{y}} \end{bmatrix} \\
\vec{X} &= \begin{bmatrix} \tilde{x} \\ \tilde{y} \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.5}
$$

The matrix $A$ is characterized by its trace and the determinant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tau &= \text{trace}(A) = a + d \\
\Delta &= \det(A) = ad - bc
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.6}
$$

Let's try to find a solution of the convenient form:

$$
\dot{\vec{v}} = \lambda \vec{v} = A\vec{v}
\tag{V.7}
$$

This vector is called the eigenvector, $\lambda$ is the corresponding eigenvalue. [V.7] can be solved by:

$$
\det \begin{bmatrix} a - \lambda & b \\ c & d - \lambda \end{bmatrix} = 0
\tag{V.8}
$$

leading to:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda_1 &= \frac{\tau + \sqrt{\tau^2 - 4\Delta}}{2} \\
\lambda_2 &= \frac{\tau - \sqrt{\tau^2 - 4\Delta}}{2}
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.9}
$$

or

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta &= \lambda_1 \lambda_2 \\
\tau &= \lambda_1 + \lambda_2
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.10}
$$

For a stable fixed point both $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$ should be negative. Therefore a stable fixed point is characterized by:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta &> 0 \\
\tau &< 0
\end{aligned}
\tag{V.11}
$$

